## Programming

Use CSS variables.

Use the npm package.

{/snippet}

{#snippet oklch()}

> [!note]
> ## What is OKLCH?
>
> OKLCH is a way to map colors in three dimensions. One dimension is lightness (white to black), one is chroma (grey to vibrant), and one is hue (through all the colors of the rainbow). The "OK" prefix is a reference to the fact that the color space does an "okay" job of mapping colors.
>
> The goal of OKLCH is to create "perceptual uniformity" between colors. So two colors with the same "lightness" value will look like they are just as light as each other. For example, here are two colors in a conventional color space (HSL) with the same "lightness" value:
> <table><tbody><tr><td style="--color: hsl(0, 100%, 50%);"></td><td style="--color: hsl(180, 100%, 50%);"></td></tr></tbody></table>
>
> The red looks much darker than the blue.
>
> Now lets try the same experiment in OKLCH:
> <table><tbody><tr><td style="--color: oklch(50% 1 0);"></td><td style="--color: oklch(50% 1 180);"></td></tr></tbody></table>
>
> These two colors are much closer in lightness. Similarly, if we were to change the chroma (AKA "saturation") or hue, we would find a very consistent shift. This is ideal for creating color schemes, as it ensures that the various colors in your scheme maintain a consistent feeling.

{/snippet}

